# Git Workflow

Apply when helping with **local Git operations**, **commit/PR wording**, or **post-merge cleanup**.

## Conventional Commits

Follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) for **git commit subjects** and **PR titles**:

```
<type>[optional scope]: <subject>
```

- **Type** (required): lowercase — `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`.
- **Scope** (optional): short noun — e.g. `feat(opentherm):` — omit if it adds noise.
- **Subject**: imperative ("add" not "added"), **no trailing period**, target **≤ 72 characters**.
- **Breaking change**: `!` after type/scope and/or footer `BREAKING CHANGE: …`.
- **Body** (optional): blank line after subject; explain *why*; wrap ~72 chars.
- **Footer** (optional): `Refs #123`, `Closes #45`, `Co-authored-by:`, etc.

**CI:** `.github/workflows/conventional-commits.yaml` requires a Conventional Commits **PR title** and Conventional Commits subjects on every **non-merge** commit in `base…head`. Git merge commits are ignored by the commits job.

**PR body** sections:

- **Summary** — what changed and why
- **Test plan** — how verified
- **Related issues** (optional) — only when the user provides or clearly implies an issue key

## Propose a commit

1. Inspect: `git status`, `git diff`, `git diff --staged`.
2. Stage coherent units; prefer **one logical change per commit**.
3. Draft the message from the actual diff.
4. Verify: type, imperative subject, ≤ 72 chars, no trailing period.

## Fix last commit / history

- **Amend** message or forgotten files only when allowed by project/user amend rules; avoid amending pushed shared history unless the user wants a force-with-lease rewrite.
- **Interactive rebase** only on local/personal feature branches before opening/updating a PR.
- Do not rewrite published `main`/`develop` without team agreement.
- Rebase/merge from the target branch per project convention; `--force-with-lease` only after an intentional history rewrite on a remote-backed branch.

## After a successful PR merge

Delete the feature branch on **remote and local** once the PR is merged (unless the user asks to keep it):

- Prefer `gh pr merge … --delete-branch` when merging via `gh`.
- If already merged: `git push origin --delete <branch>` and `git branch -d <branch>` (use `-D` only if local is fully merged remotely / user confirms).
- Switch off the feature branch first (e.g. to `main`) before deleting the local branch.
- Never delete `main`/`master`/`develop` or other long-lived protected branches.

## Examples

```
feat(opentherm): add boiler status polling

Poll TBoiler every 1s via OpenTherm data ID 25; log on change only.
```

```
fix(wifi): retry connection after DHCP timeout
```

```
feat(opentherm)!: replace legacy status enum with bit flags

BREAKING CHANGE: status field is now a u16 bitmask; update all callers.
```
